    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a report for a vineyard client in the Central Valley. Can you explain how soil pH levels in the 5.5 to 7.0 range specifically influence the availability and uptake of micronutrients like iron and manganese in grapevines? I need to make sure the chemistry part is solid.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the 5.5 to 7.0 range, soil pH affects micronutrient availability for grapevines by influencing their solubility and adsorption. In more acidic soils (pH 5.5), iron (Fe) forms insoluble ferric hydroxides, which sequester iron, making it less available to plants. Conversely, in more alkaline soils (pH 7.0), iron is more available as ferrous iron. Manganese (Mn) is less affected by pH, but its solubility decreases at higher pH levels.",
      "source": "target",
